 of the war of all against  all and so there has to be some type of  what Nietzsche is gonna call here a  peaceful agreement and what is this  peaceful agreement going to take the  form of it's not a social contract or  anything quite so developed as that as a  matter of fact on a behavioral level  it's not as if there are these these  very clear norms that everyone is  following rather Nietzsche is going to  talk first and foremost about language  and so he's going to tell us here that  this peaceful agreement is going to lead  us towards truth because what truth it  will be from now on is fixed a uniformly  valid and binding terminology for things  is invented a terminology calling this  thing this sort of thing like a shirt a  shirt and everybody else calling it a  shirt instead of a Google blop or a Zuba  Zuba or whatever or if we all want to  call this a Zuba Zuba we all get on the  same page with that and so what this is  is is you could think of it as a a sheer  dividing up of the world by use of  language that everybody else sticks to  and he says the legislation of language  also enacts the first laws of truth now  why is that well the the opposition to  truth is going to be lying and the liar  as Nietzsche says is not just anybody  any old person who says something that  is untrue it's going to be things that  are in some way parasitical and  perverting the common language using it  in some sort of inordinate or selfish or  even sometimes just creative way but  there's something that turns to one's  own advantage and to the disadvantage or  the harm of other people so the liar is  somebody who doesn't just tell a story  that isn't you know factually true it's  somebody who tells a story and get  something out of it and disadvantages or  harms somebody else in the process so he  he goes down and he says the liar uses  the valid terms the words to make the  unreal appear real so the liar is able  to tell lies by using the very shared  language the very truth that we have  over here as part of this peaceful  agreement right the liar  is able to use the terminology and then  they they do they use that to make the  unreal as Nietzsche says appear real for  example there's a great example on each  is part I am rich when kore would be the  right term how do you lie in that way  you can just come straight out and use  the words you know saying I'm rich when  when you know you'd expect to see poor  or you could gussy up your house or you  could dress in a certain way or you  could behave in a certain way and say  other things that are drawing again on  this shared language this entire  conventions that provide a shared  meaning across many human  consciousnesses so he says he misuses  established conventions by arbitrary  substitutions even reversals of names  now what nietzsche points out is that we  don't really have a problem with this as  such it was a matter fact in the rest of  the essay he's gonna talk about how not  only is this part of the way truth  itself works but we actually delight in  this think about how when you're  watching a movie actually here's a  here's a great example people watch  action movies and they lose themselves  in these totally far-fetched situations  that even if it says that this is a true  story guaranteed the movie version of it  is not true on most levels right and you  can think of all these conventions that  are that are built into it like you know  somebody throws a grenade you see a big  explosion and you know fire and people  flying through the air  real grenades don't actually work like  that but that's that's the sort of set  of signs that were that we use right  then you get somebody who says that's  not realistic you know they're perfectly  fine with everything else going on in  the movie but they see something  happening like snaps somebody's neck and  they're like they couldn't snap their  neck that way they're objecting to some  sort of lying going on and they they're  doing i